  {
    "episode_id": 73270,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/3/73270.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "I did. And I worked a variety of jobs and got elected to the Huntington town board in 1993. And then had the opportunity to run for Congress in 2000, did it? Not only did I not think I would win, but I don't know anybody who would think that I would win. And I did somehow. And so Dower were my own prospects for re-election because it was a very Republican district and I was a Democrat, still am. But I was so dower about my prospects for re-election and so cheap a human being that I refused to buy a dresser for my clothing in Washington. Because I figured I'm not getting elected. I'm not coming back here in two years. Why would I want to spend like 600 bucks on a piece of furniture that I was just up there called to the curve? But I did get re-elected and continued to get re-elected. I served 16 years and then finally left in rare form, unindicted and undefeated,",
