    "turn_idx": 38,
    "turn_text": "that i think you know look i'd never pretend to be the smartest member of congress although frankly the competition was all that stuff but uh... i know my history uh... i i'd write in a book on being one of the most studious or one of the deepest students of history. Red and red and red would walk through the Capitol at the dead of night just soaking in the history. And I think our founders, first of all, I don't know that they were naive. I think that they were actually constructively pessimistic. They knew human flaws and frailties and designed a constitution that would be able to anticipate those flaws. So before we elected our first president, the constitution was written to impeach one. How about that? before we passed up first law the constitution was written to require that it's got to get passed in the house and also the senate and get signed by the president. And if the president vetoed, it could be overridden. And then the whole thing could go to the Supreme Court for final disposition. So I think they actually were quite pessimistic in their view of humankind, but optimistic that they could create a system that would provide the institutions of democracy. I now worry deeply about our political state and status. Everybody reveres the Constitution, except when it becomes inconvenient. And so, for example, forgive me if I sound too partisan, but the Constitution gives us a system to elect presidents. You would think we would all agree on that process, except that we have people in America who say, even though the president was elected, it was rigged, it was fake, we're going to throw out the system and make these wild claims that no court in America, Republican or Democratic judge, has upheld. So I think the Constitution is really under assault. We all revere it, but we turn on it when it doesn't necessarily fulfill our own view of ideology. And I'm concerned that it's going to be challenged. One of the reasons that I open up a bookstore, because instead of pointing fingers to, you know, and blaming other people for the erosion of democracy, I believe that reading books and understanding history and literature, that will strengthen democracy.",
