    "turn_idx": 44,
    "turn_text": "Well, here's what gives me hope. And I don't want to sound too flippant or mawkish, but this is what gives me hope. I teach at Cornell University. And the reason that I teach at Cornell University, frankly, is because when I am in the classroom interacting with students, I am eternally optimistic. My students at Cornell, and I would imagine at Nassau Community College, A, they have an intolerance for intolerance. B, they're critical thinkers. You can't just say it's night when it's day or day when it's night. They will challenge you. If you have an opinion, they want to know what backs up your opinion. And they are not interested in cheap political stunts. They want to know what can be done to, as you say, or as you alluded to, make the country a little better for them and their kids, right? And so I teach not because, believe me, it's not for the money, I teach because it's my inspiration. So I have a very, I'm sorry to say, a very dystopic view of the next five to seven years. but a fundamentally right view of what happens when students at Nassau Community and students at Cornell and others enter the workforce, enter public service, politics. As one of my students said to me, I should have given this person a D minus for this. You know, you guys, meaning, you know, members of Congress, you guys have screwed it up as much as can be screwed up. Now, step aside and let us take it from here because we know what needs to be done. Some would have been offended by that. That I was heartened by that because I think that student is right.",
