    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/3/73270.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 58,
    "turn_text": "you know i think it uh... spent a lot of my time talking about this teaching it uh... pure frequently on uh... different news platforms cable uh... stations uh... i think it all comes down to eight battleground areas in the country seven battleground states and and one area in Nebraska that's where this election is going to be decided it's decided everywhere else there's no way that Donald Trump is going to win New York or get it. There's no way that Joe Biden is going to win Oklahoma or get it. The election is decided by approximately 85% of the likely electorate. It rests with the 15% in those battleground areas. These are swing voters. They can vote for either Biden or Trump. They tend to make their decisions very, very late after Halloween or after Labor Day in October. And they tend to vote based on what they're thinking at the time. So I promise you, Bill, that those independent voters, they didn't wake up this morning thinking about the Hunter Biden verdict. They don't wake up in the morning thinking about Donald Trump's latest raving. They wake up in the morning thinking about the economy, jobs, traffic, weather. That's who will decide this election. And the polls up to now don't really reflect that reality. So I think this is going to be very tight until election day and maybe the days immediately following election day.",
