    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/3/73270.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 60,
    "turn_text": "It's dysfunctional and I can explain why I think when I was elected to Congress in 2020, there were about 125 congressional districts that were in the middle where the voters wanted you to be bipartisan. They wanted you to cross the aisle. They valued moderation and they rejected extremes. Today, as you and I had this conversation, The number has been reduced to about 22 congressional districts. All of the other congressional districts have been gerrymandered, meaning drawn, or there to the left and further to the right. And so here's the news flash for listeners to your show. Most members of Congress don't wake up in the morning fearing defeat by the other party. They wake up in the morning fearing defeat from within their own party. That somebody further to the left or further to the right of them will challenge them in a primary. It means why so many members of Congress now have, if not drifted, leapt to the fringes. They're protecting themselves from their own party. And that's why it seems so hard to get any sense of agreement in Congress on virtually anything other than how we name post offices. What's the fix? Is there one? Yeah, the fix is two fundamental reforms that I've been pursuing. One is to prohibit partisan gerrymandering. We shouldn't be letting state legislators and governors draw districts to protect the incumbents of their party. That's how we got into this mess. We should have nonpartisan, independent, redistricting processes without a political agenda. So that's one reform that I think is vitally important. And the other, and I've been outspoken on this, I did a piece that went viral when I left Congress called Confessions of a Congressman in the New York Times, where I talked about the desperate need for reform of campaign finance",
